<Page {...meta}>
  <h1>How to Use AI to Plan Your Marathon Pace</h1>
  <div class="postmeta">October 13, 2026 · Training</div>

  <article>
    <p>This site has no chatbot and never will. The calculator does the math in
    your browser and nothing you enter leaves it. But plenty of runners also
    use an AI assistant to think through training, and used well, the two tools
    complement each other. Here is the workflow that works, and the places it
    goes wrong.</p>

    <h2>Step 1: Let the calculator pick the pace</h2>
    <p>Start with the part a calculator is built for. Enter the race and the
    time you are dreaming about and look at the pace per mile or kilometer.
    Then use the race equivalency tool to see what a recent race predicts, and
    set the runner profile slider honestly: did the long runs happen or not?</p>
    <p>Here is a worked example. A runner with a 1:45:00 half marathon wants to
    run 3:45:00. The same half predicts very different marathons depending on
    training:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Endurance monster (1.04): 3:34:28</li>
      <li>Textbook (1.06): 3:38:55</li>
      <li>Light on long runs (1.09): 3:45:46</li>
      <li>Fades over distance (1.12): 3:52:50</li>
    </ul>
    <p>So 3:45 is realistic for this runner only if the training looks like the
    1.09 row or better. That one number, <strong>8:35 per mile</strong>, is now
    the thing to test.</p>
    <div class="plan">
      <h3>The plan to test</h3>
      <p class="stats">3:45:00 goal &middot; 8:35 per mile &middot; half marathon 1:45:00 &middot; profile 1.09</p>
      <a class="plan-btn" href="/?d=marathon&amp;u=mi&amp;mode=even&amp;t=3:45:00&amp;eq=half&amp;eqt=1:45:00&amp;profile=1.090">Open this plan in the calculator</a>
    </div>

    <h2>Step 2: Hand the plan to AI as a skeptic</h2>
    <p>The calculator tells you what the numbers say. It cannot tell you
    whether your last six weeks of running back that up. This is where AI is
    useful: as a skeptical training partner that asks what you have not
    considered. Give it the plan and your honest background, then ask it to
    argue against you.</p>
    <blockquote class="prompt">
      <p>I want to run a 3:45 marathon, which is 8:35 per mile. My best recent
      race is a 1:45 half marathon. My longest run in the last 8 weeks was 14
      miles and I average 25 miles a week. Be a skeptical coach. What is
      realistic and what is not? What would have to be true for 8:35 to work,
      and what would you change about the plan?</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>Notice what is in that prompt: the goal, the evidence, and a request to
    push back. Without that last part, most assistants are agreeable and tell
    you the goal sounds great.</p>

    <h2>Step 3: Ask for the fade, not just the finish</h2>
    <p>Most marathon goals fail in the last six miles, so ask about that
    directly.</p>
    <blockquote class="prompt">
      <p>Given my training, where in the race am I most likely to slow down,
      and by how much? Suggest a segment plan with a controlled start, and tell
      me which miles to watch.</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>Take the answer back to the calculator and build it as segments. Now you
    can see the exact splits, the finish time, and whether the fade you planned
    still gets you to the goal. If it does not, you have just learned that
    before race day instead of during it.</p>

    <h2>Where AI goes wrong</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Arithmetic.</strong> Assistants are fluent and still make
      math mistakes, especially with times in h:mm:ss. Take pace and finish
      math from the calculator, not from the chat.</li>
      <li><strong>Agreement.</strong> If you only ask for encouragement, you
      get it. Ask it to find the weak point in your plan.</li>
      <li><strong>Your body.</strong> It cannot see your injury history,
      your sleep, or how that last long run really felt. It is not a doctor.
      Anything involving pain, heart symptoms, or medical risk belongs with a
      professional.</li>
      <li><strong>Confidence.</strong> It will sound sure even when it is
      guessing. Treat the output as questions to take to your own training
      log, not as orders.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>A note on privacy</h2>
    <p>Run times and weekly mileage are enough for pace planning. You do not
    need to paste names, home locations, or health records into a chat to get
    useful help, and it is worth checking what your assistant does with what
    you share.</p>

    <p>The short version: the calculator gives you the pace you want, and AI
    helps you decide whether you have earned it. Together they are better than
    either alone.</p>
